{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as the non-inductive basis for preferring better-corroborated theories, but it fails to address the Tichý/Miller proof which demonstrates that verisimilitude cannot be formally defined or compared in rich languages without collapsing into probability. By asserting verisimilitude as a viable mechanism while ignoring this fatal formal objection explicitly raised in the problem's counterconditions, the target's argument is internally inconsistent and fails to provide a defensible case on Popper's own rigorous terms.","premise":"If the Tichý/Miller proof holds that verisimilitude cannot be formally defined or compared in rich languages, then the appeal to verisimilitude collapses as a justification.","successor_question":"How can Popperian critical rationalism justify theory preference without relying on verisimilitude if the concept is formally undefinable?","target":"8313412ab8109c783bb0f0e488c2bb7ddfafb95d04ca749562b47e510bde5784"}]}